const body = `<h1>Loops + Strings</h1>

<p>
    Strings are sequences of characters. Loops let you visit every character one at
    a time — <strong>string traversal</strong>. You can search, count, transform, and
    validate text.
</p>

<h2>Accessing Characters</h2>

<p>
    <code>substring(i, i + 1)</code> returns the character at position <code>i</code>,
    as a one-character String. Index starts at 0, and the second argument is exclusive,
    so the slice is exactly one character wide. This is the version to reach for: it is
    on the <a href="../../exam/java-quick-reference.html">Java Quick Reference</a>, and
    it hands you a String, which is what the sheet's other methods expect.
</p>

<pre><code>String weapon = "Longsword";
// Index:           012345678

weapon.substring(0, 1);  // "L"
weapon.substring(4, 5);  // "s"
weapon.substring(8, 9);  // "d"</code></pre>

<p>
    Java also has <code>charAt(index)</code>, which returns a <code>char</code> instead
    of a String, and that method is not on the sheet. It shows up all over this unit's
    code because it is shorter to type, so learn to read it, and write the
    <code>substring</code> form when the exam asks:
</p>

<pre><code>weapon.charAt(0);  // 'L'. Java only, not on the sheet
weapon.charAt(4);  // 's'
weapon.charAt(8);  // 'd'</code></pre>

<p>
    Two things change when you switch to the sheet version. The result is a String, so
    you compare it with <code>equals</code> (<code>weapon.substring(0, 1).equals("L")</code>)
    rather than <code>==</code>. And the last index is still <code>length() - 1</code>,
    so the off-by-one rules do not move.
</p>

<div class="callout lab bug-hunt">
    <h3>🐛 Bug Hunt: Out of Bounds</h3>
    <p>A student wrote this code to print the last letter of a weapon's name:</p>
<pre><code>String weapon = "Longsword";
char lastLetter = weapon.charAt(weapon.length());
System.out.println(lastLetter);</code></pre>
    <p><strong>Your Task:</strong> Why does this code throw a <code>StringIndexOutOfBoundsException</code>? What exact change needs to be made to fix it?</p>
    <p>The sheet version fails the same way, and for the same reason:
    <code>weapon.substring(weapon.length(), weapon.length() + 1)</code> also asks for a
    character that is not there. The boundary rule belongs to the index, not to the
    method.</p>
</div>

<h2>String Traversal Pattern</h2>

<pre><code>String spell = "Fireball";

for (int i = 0; i < spell.length(); i++) {
    char c = spell.charAt(i);
    System.out.println("Position " + i + ": " + c);
}</code></pre>

<p>The same loop with the sheet's tool, which returns each character as a String:</p>

<pre><code>for (int i = 0; i &lt; spell.length(); i++) {
    String c = spell.substring(i, i + 1);
    System.out.println("Position " + i + ": " + c);
}</code></pre>

<p class="output">Position 0: F<br>Position 1: i<br>Position 2: r<br>Position 3: e<br>Position 4: b<br>Position 5: a<br>Position 6: l<br>Position 7: l</p>

<h2>Counting Characters</h2>

<pre><code>// How many 'l's in "Fireball"?
String spell = "Fireball";
int count = 0;

for (int i = 0; i < spell.length(); i++) {
    if (spell.charAt(i) == 'l') {
        count++;
    }
}
System.out.println("Found " + count + " 'l' characters.");
// → "Found 2 'l' characters."</code></pre>

<p>
    On the sheet that test line becomes
    <code>if (spell.substring(i, i + 1).equals("l"))</code>. The loop itself does not
    change, only the comparison, because a one-character String is still a String and
    Strings compare with <code>equals</code>.
</p>

<h2>Building Strings in a Loop</h2>

<p>
    Strings are immutable — you can't change them. Instead, <strong>build a new one</strong>.
</p>

<pre><code>// Reverse a string
String original = "sword";
String reversed = "";

for (int i = original.length() - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
    reversed += original.charAt(i);
    // on the sheet, append the one-character String instead:
    // reversed += original.substring(i, i + 1);
}
System.out.println(reversed);  // "drows"</code></pre>

<div class="callout lab socratic">
    <h3>🧠 Socratic Checkpoint: The Memory Cost</h3>
    <p>Strings in Java are <em>immutable</em>, meaning they can never be changed once created. Every time you use <code>+=</code>, Java creates a brand new String and throws the old one away.</p>
    <p><strong>Think about it:</strong> If you use a loop to build a String of 1,000 characters one by one using <code>+=</code>, how many temporary Strings does Java have to create and destroy? (Hint: Later, you'll learn about <code>StringBuilder</code> which solves this problem!)</p>
    <p>One flag on <code>StringBuilder</code>: it is not on the Java Quick Reference
    either. The sheet's tool for assembling a String is the <code>+=</code> you are using
    right now, so on exam day the loop you already have is the answer, and
    <code>StringBuilder</code> is the performance upgrade you reach for in your own
    programs.</p>
</div>

<h2>Application: RPG Word Filter</h2>

<pre><code>// Check if a spell name starts with "Fire"
String spell = "Fireball";

if (spell.length() >= 4) {
    String prefix = "";
    for (int i = 0; i < 4; i++) {
        prefix += spell.charAt(i);
    }
    if (prefix.equals("Fire")) {
        System.out.println("FIRE spell detected! Boost damage.");
    }
}</code></pre>

<p>Easier way (once you know substring), and the version that runs on exam day, since
<code>charAt</code> is not on the sheet:</p>

<pre><code>if (spell.substring(0, 4).equals("Fire")) { ... }</code></pre>

<h2>Finding Substrings</h2>

<pre><code>// Does the password contain "dragon"?
String password = "red-dragon-gate";
String keyword = "dragon";
boolean found = false;

for (int i = 0; i <= password.length() - keyword.length(); i++) {
    String piece = password.substring(i, i + keyword.length());
    if (piece.equals(keyword)) {
        found = true;
        break;  // stop — found it
    }
}</code></pre>

<h2>Application: Combat Command Parser</h2>

<pre><code>Scanner input = new Scanner(System.in);
System.out.print("Action: ");
String cmd = input.nextLine();

// Parse "attack goblin" into verb and target
int spacePos = -1;
for (int i = 0; i < cmd.length(); i++) {
    if (cmd.charAt(i) == ' ') {
        spacePos = i;
        break;
    }
}
// The sheet finds the same position in one call:
// int spacePos = cmd.indexOf(" ");   // -1 when there is no space

if (spacePos == -1) {
    System.out.println("One-word command: " + cmd);
} else {
    String verb = cmd.substring(0, spacePos);
    String target = cmd.substring(spacePos + 1);
    System.out.println("Verb: \\"" + verb + "\\" → Target: \\"" + target + "\\"");
}</code></pre>

<h2>Character Testing Methods</h2>

<p>These <code>Character</code> class methods test what kind of character something is.
None of the five is on the Java Quick Reference: the sheet has no
<code>Character</code> class at all.</p>

<table>
    <tr><th>Method</th><th>True for...</th></tr>
    <tr><td><code>Character.isDigit(c)</code></td><td>'0'–'9'</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>Character.isLetter(c)</code></td><td>'a'–'z', 'A'–'Z'</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>Character.isUpperCase(c)</code></td><td>'A'–'Z'</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>Character.isLowerCase(c)</code></td><td>'a'–'z'</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>Character.isWhitespace(c)</code></td><td>space, tab, newline</td></tr>
</table>

<p>
    The sheet's tool for the same question is <code>compareTo</code> on a String, so each
    test becomes a range check on a one-character String:
</p>

<pre><code>String ch = name.substring(i, i + 1);      // one character, as a String
boolean isDigit  = ch.compareTo("0") &gt;= 0 &amp;&amp; ch.compareTo("9") &lt;= 0;
boolean isLetter = (ch.compareTo("a") &gt;= 0 &amp;&amp; ch.compareTo("z") &lt;= 0)
                || (ch.compareTo("A") &gt;= 0 &amp;&amp; ch.compareTo("Z") &lt;= 0);</code></pre>

<p>
    Uppercase and lowercase sit in two separate runs, which is why the letter test needs
    both halves. <code>isUpperCase</code> is the first half alone, <code>isLowerCase</code>
    the second, and <code>isWhitespace</code> covers space, tab, and newline, so where you
    are testing for a space the substitute is <code>ch.equals(" ")</code>.
</p>

<p>Here is the counting loop twice, Java first, then the sheet:</p>

<pre><code>// Count only the letters in a player name
String name = "Gandalf99";
int letters = 0;
for (int i = 0; i < name.length(); i++) {
    if (Character.isLetter(name.charAt(i))) {
        letters++;
    }
}</code></pre>

<pre><code>// The same loop with nothing but sheet methods
int letters = 0;
for (int i = 0; i &lt; name.length(); i++) {
    String ch = name.substring(i, i + 1);
    if ((ch.compareTo("a") &gt;= 0 &amp;&amp; ch.compareTo("z") &lt;= 0)
     || (ch.compareTo("A") &gt;= 0 &amp;&amp; ch.compareTo("Z") &lt;= 0)) {
        letters++;
    }
}</code></pre>

<h2>Tinker Challenge: The Cipher</h2>

<div class="callout lab tinker">
    <h3>🛠️ Tinker Challenge: Vowel Counter & Parser</h3>
    <p>Create a tool that analyzes magical incantations (Strings).</p>
    <ul>
        <li>Ask the user to enter a spell name.</li>
        <li>Write a loop that traverses the string and counts the total number of vowels (A, E, I, O, U). Make sure it works for both uppercase and lowercase!</li>
        <li><strong>Bonus:</strong> Modify your loop so it also counts how many numbers (digits) are in the spell name, using the <code>Character.isDigit()</code> method (or, on exam day, the <code>compareTo</code> range check above).</li>
    </ul>
    <p><em>Testing:</em> If the user types "F1r3bAll", what should the vowel count be? What should the digit count be?</p>
</div>


